  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14185522/assets/4234a330c5f497e3eae5af5006b87197f6911114f8b104b9050223d08ae7d310.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, what is the length of the shorter leg?",
  "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14185522/assets/b8f2ba89d30a41072f635916eb07b394fd4bd83eb39181f13e852d1b776de5ee.png)\n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $7\\sqrt{3},$ and the shorter leg has length $x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is $7.$",
